  "question": "In a lottery, there are 10 prizes and 90 blanks. A ticket is drawn at random. Find the probability that:<br />(a) the ticket is a prize,<br />(b) if 5 tickets are drawn, what is the probability that exactly one of them is a prize?<br />(c) What is the probability that at least one is a prize?",
  "options": {
    "A": "(a) $\\frac{1}{10}$<br />(b) $0.32805$<br />(c) $\\frac{40951}{100000}$",
    "B": "(a) $\\frac{9}{10}$<br />(b) $\\frac{\\binom{90}{1}\\binom{10}{4}}{\\binom{100}{5}}$<br />(c) $\\frac{\\binom{90}{5}}{\\binom{100}{5}}$",
    "C": "(a) $\\frac{1}{10}$<br />(b) $\\frac{\\binom{10}{1}\\binom{90}{4}}{\\binom{100}{5}}=\\frac{12905}{38024}$<br />(c) $1-\\frac{\\binom{90}{5}}{\\binom{100}{5}}=\\frac{79137}{190120}$",
    "D": "(a) $\\frac{10}{90}$<br />(b) $\\frac{\\binom{10}{5}}{\\binom{100}{5}}$<br />(c) $\\frac{\\binom{10}{1}}{\\binom{100}{1}}$"
